            <ArticleTitle>Response of severely obese children and adolescents to behavioral treatment.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1103-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1001/2013.jamapediatrics.319</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVES" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To investigate whether the degree of obesity predicts the efficacy of long-term behavioral treatment and to explore any interaction with age.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">A 3-year longitudinal observational study. Obese children were divided into 3 age groups (6-9, 10-13, and 14-16 years) and also into 2 groups (moderately obese, with a body mass index [BMI]-standard deviation [SD] score [or z score] of 1.6 to &lt;3.5, and severely obese, with a BMI-SD score of ≥3.5).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SETTING" NlmCategory="METHODS">National Childhood Obesity Center, Stockholm, Sweden.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PARTICIPANTS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Children 6 to 16 years of age who started treatment between 1998 and 2006.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="INTERVENTION" NlmCategory="METHODS">Behavioral treatment of obesity.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MAIN OUTCOME MEASURE" NlmCategory="METHODS">Change in BMI-SD score during 3 years of treatment; a reduction in BMI-SD score of 0.5 units or more was defined as clinically significant.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">A total of 643 children (49% female children) met the inclusion criteria. Among the youngest moderately obese children, 44% had a clinically significant reduction in BMI-SD score (mean reduction, -0.4 [95% CI, -0.55 to -0.32]). Treatment was less effective for the older moderately obese children. Twenty percent of children who were 10 to 13 years of age and 8% of children who were 14 to 16 years of age had a reduction in BMI-SD score of 0.5 units or more; 58% of the severely obese young children showed a clinically significant reduction in BMI-SD score (mean reduction, -0.7 [95% CI, -0.80 to -0.54]). The severely obese adolescents showed no change in mean BMI-SD score after 3 years, and 2% experienced clinically significant weight loss. Age was found to be a predictor of a reduction in BMI-SD score (odds ratio, 0.68 units per year [95% CI, 0.60-0.77 units per year]).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Behavioral treatment was successful for severely obese children but had almost no effect on severely obese adolescents.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
